Derive the surface circulation identity from a parameter patch (@math-fields-stokes: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Localize-an-integral-balance-with-explicit-regularity-and-domain.lingua#r_0083DE48564035390466617B57") { r_00FB68AFB25B0AA0ABA7407DAB
Let φ map a rectangle U injectively to a regular surface patch, with φ∈C² near U, and let F∈C¹ near its image. Choose orientation φ_u×φ_v. Its positively oriented boundary is the image of the counterclockwise parameter boundary. Define A(u,v)=F(φ)·φ_u and B(u,v)=F(φ)·φ_v. The chain rule turns the surface boundary circulation into ∮_∂U A du+B dv. The proved rectangle identity gives ∬_U(B_u−A_v)du dv.

Differentiate the pairings: B_u−A_v=(DF(φ)φ_u)·φ_v−(DF(φ)φ_v)·φ_u+F(φ)·(φ_vu−φ_uv). The last term is zero by the C² mixed-partial theorem. Expanding the remaining two products and collecting antisymmetric component pairs gives (curlF)(φ)·(φ_u×φ_v). Substitution is exactly the oriented surface integral. This proves Stokes' identity on the stated rectangle patch. It also shows why inconsistent boundary/surface orientations cannot be fixed by changing only the name of the normal.

Finite oriented patch unions follow when internal boundary curves agree and have opposite induced orientations. The general theorem for piecewise smooth orientable surfaces, with compatible boundary orientation and a C¹ field on a neighborhood of the surface, is accepted beyond this patch proof. Read [Calculus and differential-equation preparation — Strang and Herman, OpenStax](../../chemistry/industrial-chemistry/sources/Calculus-and-Differential-Equations-OpenStax.lingua#r_027WKVM5NG5X84M86TSVRA77J3), Volume 3 section 6.7 Theorem 6.19. Surfaces with several boundary components require the induced orientation on each; a field singularity on the chosen spanning surface prevents the application even if the boundary curve avoids it.

For φ=(u,v,hu+kv+quv) and F=(−a y²,b x²,cxy), the curl is (cx,−cy,2bx+2ay). Dotting with the oriented area vector (−h−qv,−k−qu,1) cancels the two quv terms and leaves (2b−ch)u+(2a+ck)v. On [0,2]×[−1,1], a=1,b=2,c=3,h=2,k=−1,q=1, the surface result is −8. Direct boundary parameterization gives edge integrals −8,16,−16,0, also totaling −8. The graph is nonplanar when q≠0, so this is more than a flat-square check.

**Work:** repeat with q=0 and q=3 and explain why this family's answer is unchanged although the surfaces and their boundary curves change. It is the computed cancellation here, not a claim that all deformations preserve circulation. Reverse both orientations and obtain +8. Set h=k=q=c=0 to recover the planar example.

